targets have q d predecessors. Exactly q targets have the largest fibre size
q m−1 ; they are
                        (−t + ctm−1 , 0),   c ∈ Fq .                   (3.3)
The image size is
                                                   m−2
                                                   X
                          | im F | = q + (q − 1)         q 2j .                  (3.4)
                                                   j=1

All remaining targets have empty fibres. An empty sum is zero.

Proof. Translation u 7→ t + u permutes I. For each ordinary stratum 1 ≤
d ≤ m − 2, exactly (q − 1)q m−d−1 values of u give valuation d. For each
such u, there are q m−d−1 admissible values of w. Their product is (3.2).
The saturated condition t + u ∈ tm−1 R instead has the q solutions in (3.3),
each permitting only w = 0. These fibres are larger than all ordinary ones.
Summing their counts gives (3.4). At m = 2 there is no ordinary stratum:
all q reachable targets have q predecessors.


4    Multiplication and linear controls
The fibre theorem is an evaluated multiplication relation. On the same
carrier I 2 , define

    M (a, b) = (b, ab),      P (x, y) = (x, y + t),          Q(u, w) = (u − t, w).

Both P and Q are bijections, and direct substitution gives

                                  F = Q ◦ M ◦ P.                                 (4.1)

Thus every one-step fibre and image count transfers from multiplication. No
new inverse mechanism is claimed. Since Q ̸= P −1 , this two-sided identity
is not an iterate conjugacy and does not supply the temporal proof.

Proposition 4.1 (Same clock without endomorphism conjugacy). The lin-
ear map L : I 2 → I 2 , L(x, y) = (y, tx), has the same pointwise clock and
depth census as F . If m = 2, then F = L. If m ≥ 3, F is not bijectively
conjugate to any endomorphism of a finite group.

Proof. For k ≥ 0,

            L2k (x, y) = (tk x, tk y),    L2k+1 (x, y) = (tk y, tk+1 x).



                                         5
These formulas give (1.3) and hence Corollary 2.3. When m = 2, every
product xy with x, y ∈ I vanishes, so F = L.
    For m ≥ 3, Corollary 3.2 supplies nonempty fibres of distinct sizes q
and q m−1 . If ϕ : G → G is a finite-group endomorphism and ϕ(g) = h,
then ϕ−1 ({h}) = g ker ϕ: applying ϕ to g −1 z proves both inclusions. All
its nonempty fibres therefore have the same size. A bijective conjugacy
preserves fibre cardinalities, giving the contradiction.

    The obstruction concerns endomorphisms, not arbitrary nonlinear fac-
tors. Equal clocks and equal depth distributions are therefore weaker than
a conjugacy in this family; their common numerical shape is not itself the
advance of Theorem 2.2.


5    Verification scope and limitations
The proofs above hold for every prime power q and every m ≥ 2. The accom-
panying author-verifier source is restricted to q ∈ {2, 3, 4} and m ∈ {2, 3, 4}.
It is designed to enumerate every state, its literal transition and orbit-derived
depth, and the full predecessor set of every target, before checking the dis-
played formulas. For q = 4 the coefficient field is F2 [α]/(α2 + α + 1), not
arithmetic modulo four. At this source-draft stage, no execution or compu-
tational result is claimed.
     The result is specific to (1.1) on the full ideal square I 2 . It establishes
neither an arbitrary-chain-ring extension nor all-time fibre formulas or a
classification of functional-graph isomorphisms. The temporal conclusion is
narrower: leading-term cancellation changes one register’s history without
changing the exact deadline of the full nonlinear state.


